Yes, in Java String is a class (java.lang.String), but you cannot extend it because:

✅ 1️⃣ String is declared as final
The String class in Java is:

java
Copy
Edit
public final class String implements Serializable, Comparable<String>, CharSequence {
    ...
}
final means no class can inherit (extend) it.

✅ 2️⃣ Why did Java designers make it final?
Immutability

String objects are immutable. If it were subclassed, someone could override methods and make it mutable, breaking security and performance optimizations.

Security

Strings are used in sensitive places (class loading, file paths, network connections). If a subclass could modify behavior, it could introduce vulnerabilities.

Hashcode caching

String caches its hashcode because it doesn't change. If a subclass could modify contents, cached hashcode would become invalid.

Performance optimization

The JVM optimizes String heavily (intern pool, constant folding). Subclassing would break these optimizations.

✅ 3️⃣ What if you need custom behavior?
You can't extend String, but you can:

Wrap it in your own class (Composition):

java
Copy
Edit
class MyString {
    private final String value;
    MyString(String value) { this.value = value; }
}
Use StringBuilder or StringBuffer for mutable strings.

🔑 Summary:
String is a class, but final prevents extension.

This ensures immutability, security, and performance.